I built and saved the workflow, but I could not confirm it starts a call. The one-turn chat check returned a 504 gateway timeout twice. That is not the 500 that would mean the workflow is broken, but the start is still untested. No practice cases were run.

The lint check and the situations check both found no problems, and the save went through. It is the full shape from routing.md:

- **Flow:** your date-facts code node as the start node, then the opening, identity checks and a silent account lookup.
- **Account hook:** your hook code is set verbatim on `get_account`.
- **Router and negotiation:** a router states the debt and asks "Can you make this payment today?". All the promise rules live in a separate negotiation node.
- **Specialists:** the six situations each have their own node, every node closes through `close_call` to the hang-up node, and DATE FACTS is in every node that deals with dates.

There were no extra date or amount rules to write in by hand: the list of rules not computed in code was empty.

I changed the trigger for `cannot_pay_refusal`, and the brief backs this. The brief (rules 30–31) says a flat refusal uses up a persuasion attempt and the call closes only once all attempts are used. So the negotiation node now makes the attempts itself, and the call moves to `cannot_pay_refusal` only when the attempts are used up and the customer still refuses, or says twice they don't want to talk. The extracted version would have sent the first refusal straight there.

The generator is `out/w4-A-a1.gen.py` and the workflow is `out/w4-A-a1.workflow.json`.

